Using the tools from \Cref{sec:surj-hard-inputs} and the above discussion, we can now construct a polynomial that corresponds to the informal description in \Cref{alg:SURJalmost}:  egin{equation} r(x) =  rac{1}{ inom{N}{S}} \sum_{\mathcal{S} \in  inom{[N]}{S}} \sum_{y \in [R]^S} {\mathds{1}}_y(x_{\mathcal{S}}) p_{\mathcal{R}(y)}(x). \end{equation} This is a polynomial of degree $S\log R + \max_{y}\{\deg(p_{\mathcal{R}(y)})\} = 	ilde{O}(S + \sqrt{NT}) = 	ilde{O}(N^{3/4})$, using $S=	ilde{\Theta}(N^{3/4})$ and $T=	ilde{\Theta}(\sqrt{N})$ (where the factors hidden by the $	ilde{\Theta}$ notation will be chosen later). The value of the polynomial on input $x$ is  egin{equation} r(x) =  rac{1}{ inom{N}{S}} \sum_{\mathcal{S} \in  inom{[N]}{S}} p_{\mathcal{R}(x_{\mathcal{S}})}(x). \end{equation} where recall that $\mathcal{R}(x_{\mathcal{S}})$ is as defined in Equation \eqref{nomoreeqs}. The right hand side of Equation \eqref{fleck} is precisely the expected value of the polynomial $p_{\mathcal{R}(x_{\mathcal{S}})}(x)$ when $\mathcal{S}$ is a uniformly random set of size $S$. We will now show that $r(x)$ is an approximating polynomial for Surjectivity, i.e., that for all $x\in[R]^N$, $|\mathsf{ SURJ}(x)-r(x)|\leq 1/3$. Recalling that $b_{\mathcal{R}(x_{\mathcal{S}})}$ is the number of range items $r \in \mathcal{R}$ that appear in $x$ greater than $T$ times (cf. Equation \eqref{eq:b}), we compute the value of the polynomial $r(x)$ on an input $x$:  egin{align} r(x) &=  rac{1}{ inom{N}{S}} \sum_{\mathcal{S} \in  inom{[N]}{S}} p_{\mathcal{R}(x_{\mathcal{S}})}(x) \ &=  rac{1}{ inom{N}{S}} \left(\sum_{\mathcal{S} \in  inom{[N]}{S}} \sum_{b=0}^{N/T} {\mathds{1}}[b_{\mathcal{R}(x_{\mathcal{S}})}(x)=b] \cdot p_{\mathcal{R}(x_{\mathcal{S}})}(x) ight)\ &=  rac{1}{ inom{N}{S}} \sum_{\mathcal{S} \in  inom{[N]}{S}} {\mathds{1}}[b_{\mathcal{R}(x_{\mathcal{S}})}(x)=0] \cdot p_{\mathcal{R}(x_{\mathcal{S}})}(x) \ &\qquad\qquad\qquad + \sum_{b=1}^{N/T}  rac{1}{ inom{N}{S}} \sum_{\mathcal{S} \in  inom{[N]}{S}} {\mathds{1}}[b_{\mathcal{R}(x_{\mathcal{S}})}(x)=b] \cdot p_{\mathcal{R}(x_{\mathcal{S}})}(x), \end{align} where we split up the sum into the $b=0$ \eqref{eq:t1} and $b\geq 1$ \eqref{eq:t2} cases. We first show that the $b\geq1$ term \eqref{eq:t2} has essentially no contribution to the final value. When $b_{\mathcal{R}(x_{\mathcal{S}})}(x) \geq 1$, we know by \Cref{lem:boundonprcal} that the magnitude of the polynomial $|p_{\mathcal{R}}(x)|$ is at most $\exp\left(b_{\mathcal{R}(x_{\mathcal{S}})}(x) \cdot 	ilde{O}\left(\sqrt{T}ight)ight)$. Thus the magnitude of the term in Expression \eqref{eq:t2} is at most  egin{align} &\sum_{b=1}^{N/T}  rac{1}{ inom{N}{S}} \sum_{\mathcal{S} \in  inom{[N]}{S}} {\mathds{1}}[b_{\mathcal{R}(x_{\mathcal{S}})}(x)=b] \cdot \exp(b \cdot 	ilde{O}(\sqrt{T})) \ =&\sum_{b=1}^{N/T} \Pr_{\mathcal{S}}[b_{\mathcal{R}(x_{\mathcal{S}})}(x)=b] \cdot \exp\left(b \cdot 	ilde{O}\left(\sqrt{T}ight)ight). \end{align} We now need to compute the value of $\Pr_{\mathcal{S}}[b_{\mathcal{R}(x_{\mathcal{S}})}(x)=b]$ for each $b$. Intuitively, this roughly corresponds to the probability that we sample $S=	ilde{\Theta}(N^{3/4})$ elements from the input and miss all $bT$ elements that correspond to the $T = 	ilde{\Theta}(\sqrt{N})$ copies of the $b$ range items that appear at least $T$ times. If we were to sample $N / (bT)$ elements, the probability of seeing none of the $b T$ elements would be $\Theta(1)$. Since we are sampling $S=	ilde{\Theta}(N^{3/4}) \gg N / (bT)$ elements, the probability of not seeing one of the $bT$ elements is ${\exp(-bST/N)} = {\exp(-b \cdot 	ilde{\Omega}(N^{1/4}))}$. We make this heuristic calculation formal in the following lemma:  egin{lemma} Let $S, T \le N$. Let $\mathcal{S} \subset [N]$ be a random subset of size $S$. Then for every $x \in [R]^N$ and every $b \ge 1$, \[\Pr_{\mathcal{S}}[b_{\mathcal{R}(x_{\mathcal{S}})}(x) \ge b] \leq {\exp(-b \cdot (ST/N - \log N))},\] recalling that $b_{\mathcal{R}(x_{\mathcal{S}})}$ is the number of range items $r \in \mathcal{R}$ that appear in $x$ more than $T$ times, but do not appear in $x_{\mathcal{S}}$. \end{lemma}  egin{proof} To calculate the probability of interest, we begin by analyzing a simplified experiment. Suppose there are $b$ range items $r_1, \dots, r_b$ each appearing greater than $T$ times in $x$. We compute the probability that \emph{none} of the items $r_1, \dots, r_b$ appear in the sample $x_{\mathcal{S}}$. If $T \ge N - S$, then this probability is zero. Otherwise, by direct calculation, we have for each $i = 1, \dots, b$: \[\Pr_{\mathcal{S}}[r_i 
